What <code>.replace()</code> Does <ul> <li>Returns a new string with the first match replaced</li> <li>Takes two arguments:</li> <li>The pattern to search for (string or regex)</li> <li>The replacement string or function</li> <li>Only replaces the first occurrence by default (unless using regex with <code>/g</code>)</li> <li>Does not modify the original string</li> </ul> <p>Syntax: <pre><code>string.replace(searchValue, newValue)
</code></pre></p> Use Cases with Examples <ul> <li> <p>Replace the first match only <code>"hello world".replace("l", "L"); // 'heLlo world'</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Substitute values in template strings <code>"My name is NAME".replace("NAME", "Alice"); // 'My name is Alice'</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Use regex for pattern matching <code>"a1b2c3".replace(/\d/, "X"); // 'aXb2c3'</code></p> </li> </ul> Common Pitfalls <ul> <li> <p>Only replaces the first match, unless using <code>/g</code> flag <code>"aaa".replace("a", "b"); // 'baa'</code> <code>"aaa".replace(/a/g, "b"); // 'bbb'"</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Be careful with special characters in regex <code>"1+1=2".replace("+", "-"); // '1-1=2'"</code>"1+1=2".replace(/+/, "-"); // safer"`</p> </li> </ul> Code Example <pre><code>const message = "bbb ccc";

// Replace the first 'b' with 'c'
let result = message.replace('b', 'c');
console.log(result); // 'cbb ccc'
</code></pre>

What <code>.replaceAll()</code> Does <ul> <li>Returns a new string with all occurrences of a substring or pattern replaced</li> <li>Supports both string values and regular expressions</li> <li>Regular expressions must include the global (<code>g</code>) flag, or it throws an error</li> </ul> <p>Syntax: <pre><code>string.replaceAll(searchValue, newValue)
</code></pre></p> Use Cases with Examples <ul> <li> <p>Clean up repeated words or characters <code>"ha ha ha".replaceAll("ha", "ho"); // "ho ho ho"</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Replace multiple occurrences in a sentence <code>"foo is foo".replaceAll("foo", "bar"); // "bar is bar"</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Use regex for complex matching <code>"123-456-7890".replaceAll(/\d/g, "*"); // "***-***-****"</code></p> </li> </ul> Common Mistakes <ul> <li>Using a RegExp without the <code>g</code> flag will throw a <code>TypeError</code></li> <li><code>replaceAll()</code> is not supported in IE (use polyfill or fallback to <code>replace</code> with regex)</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "test test";
console.log(str.replaceAll(/test/, "done")); // ❌ Error — missing 'g' flag
</code></pre> Code Example <pre><code>const str = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day.";

// Replace all "day" with "year"
console.log(str.replaceAll("day", "year"));
// Output: Carbon emission is increasing year by year.

// Using a regular expression (must include 'g')
console.log(str.replaceAll(/day/g, "month"));
// Output: Carbon emission is increasing month by month.
</code></pre>

What <code>.search()</code> Does <ul> <li>Returns the index of the first match of a substring or regex</li> <li>Returns <code>-1</code> if no match is found</li> <li>Accepts string or regular expression as input</li> </ul> <p>Syntax: <pre><code>string.search(searchValue)
</code></pre></p> Use Cases with Examples <ul> <li> <p>Find the position of a word in a sentence <code>"The sky is blue".search("blue"); // 11</code></p> </li> <li> <p>Use regex to search patterns <code>"Code123".search(/\d/); // 4</code> — finds the first digit</p> </li> <li> <p>Case-insensitive search using regex <code>"HELLO".search(/hello/i); // 0</code></p> </li> </ul> Common Mistakes <ul> <li> <p><code>search()</code> only returns index, not matched text   To get the actual match, use <code>.match()</code> instead</p> </li> <li> <p>It always finds only the first match, even if multiple exist</p> </li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "abc abc abc";
console.log(str.search("abc")); // 0 — not 4 or 8
</code></pre> Code Example <pre><code>const text = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";

const index = text.search("fox");

console.log(index); // Output: 16
</code></pre>

What <code>.split()</code> Does <ul> <li>Divides a string into an array of substrings</li> <li>Takes a separator (character, string, or RegExp)</li> <li>Optionally accepts a limit to control number of splits</li> </ul> <p>Syntax: <pre><code>string.split(separator, limit?)
</code></pre></p> Use Cases with Examples <ul> <li> <p>Split a sentence into words <pre><code>"Hello World".split(" "); // ["Hello", "World"]
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Split into characters <pre><code>"ABCDEF".split(""); // ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F"]
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Split with a custom delimiter <pre><code>"Java;Python;C++".split(";"); // ["Java", "Python", "C++"]
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Split using regular expressions <pre><code>let langs = "JavaScript ;  Python ;C;C++";
let result = langs.split(/\s*(?:;|$)\s*/);
console.log(result); // ["JavaScript", "Python", "C", "C++"]
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Limit the number of results <pre><code>"Java is fun. Java is fast.".split(".", 2); 
// ["Java is fun", " Java is fast"]
</code></pre></p> </li> </ul> Common Mistakes <ul> <li> <p>Omitting separator returns entire string as 1 array element <pre><code>"Text here".split(); // ["Text here"]
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Passing empty string splits every character <pre><code>"Hi!".split(""); // ["H", "i", "!"]
</code></pre></p> </li> </ul> Code Example <pre><code>const sentence = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day";

console.log(sentence.split());         // [ 'Carbon emission is increasing day by day' ]
console.log(sentence.split(''));       // ['C','a','r',...]
console.log(sentence.split(' '));      // ['Carbon','emission','is',...]
console.log(sentence.split('is'));     // ['Carbon emission ', ' increasing day by day']
</code></pre> <pre><code>const text = "Java is awesome. Java is fun.";

console.log(text.split("."));          // ['Java is awesome', ' Java is fun', '']
console.log(text.split(".", 2));       // ['Java is awesome', ' Java is fun']
</code></pre> <pre><code>const text2 = "JavaScript ;  Python ;C;C++";
let pattern = /\s*(?:;|$)\s*/;

console.log(text2.split(pattern));     // ['JavaScript', 'Python', 'C', 'C++']
</code></pre>

Example<pre><code>Example:
const sentence = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day";
console.log(sentence.startsWith("Car"));
console.log(sentence.startsWith("carbon")); // return false case-sensitive
</code></pre>

What <code>.substring()</code> Does <ul> <li>Extracts characters between two indices</li> <li>Returns a new string</li> <li>Does not modify the original string</li> <li>If <code>endIndex</code> is omitted, extracts till the end of the string</li> <li>If <code>startIndex &gt; endIndex</code>, it swaps them internally</li> </ul> <p>Syntax: <pre><code>string.substring(startIndex, endIndex)
</code></pre></p> <ul> <li><code>startIndex</code>: index at which to begin extraction (included)</li> <li><code>endIndex</code>: index before which to stop (excluded)</li> </ul> Use Cases with Examples <ul> <li> <p>Extract substring from index 0 to 5 <pre><code>const text = "JavaScript";
console.log(text.substring(0, 5)); // "JavaS"
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Omit end index to go till end <pre><code>console.log(text.substring(4)); // "Script"
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Automatically swaps indices <pre><code>console.log(text.substring(6, 4)); // "Sc"
</code></pre></p> </li> </ul> Common Mistakes <ul> <li> <p><code>.substring()</code> treats negative values as <code>0</code> <pre><code>console.log("hello".substring(-2)); // "hello"
</code></pre></p> </li> <li> <p>Unlike <code>.slice()</code>, it does not support negative indexes</p> </li> </ul> Code Example <pre><code>const fullText = "Hello, Angular!";

// Extract from index 7 to end
const extractedText = fullText.substring(7);
console.log(extractedText); // "Angular!"

// Extract from index 7 to 13 (not including 13)
const partialText = fullText.substring(7, 13);
console.log(partialText); // "Angular"
</code></pre>

What It Does <ul> <li>Extracts characters from <code>startIndex</code> to <code>endIndex</code> (exclusive)</li> <li>If <code>endIndex</code> is omitted, extraction continues to the end of the string</li> <li>If <code>startIndex &gt; endIndex</code>, the method automatically swaps them</li> <li>Does not include the character at <code>endIndex</code></li> </ul> Examples <pre><code>fullText: string = 'Hello, Angular!';
extractedText: string;

// Extract from index 7 to the end
this.extractedText = this.fullText.substring(7);
console.log(this.extractedText); // "Angular!"

// Extract from index 7 to 13 (not including 13)
const partialText = this.fullText.substring(7, 13);
console.log(partialText); // "Angular"
</code></pre>

Key Differences <ul> <li><code>substring(startIndex, endIndex)</code></li> <li>Extracts between two indices</li> <li><code>substr(startIndex, length)</code></li> <li>Extracts number of characters starting from an index</li> <li><code>substr()</code> supports negative index (counts from end)</li> <li><code>substr()</code> is deprecated — avoid using in modern code</li> </ul> Side-by-Side Code Examples <pre><code>let text = "JavaScript";

// Using substring
console.log(text.substring(4, 10)); // "Script"

// Using substr: extract 6 characters starting at index 4
console.log(text.substr(4, 6)); // "Script"

// Using substr with negative index
console.log(text.substr(-6, 6)); // "Script"

// Omitting length extracts to the end
console.log(text.substr(4)); // "Script"
</code></pre> Avoid Using <code>substr()</code> in New Code <p>While <code>substr()</code> may still work in some environments, it is deprecated and not recommended for long-term use.</p> <p>✅ Prefer <code>substring()</code> or <code>slice()</code> for extracting parts of strings.</p>

Example<pre><code>const str = "    TutorialsTonight    ";
console.log(str.trim());

oputput- TutorialsTonight
</code></pre>

What It Does <ul> <li>Returns the primitive string value of a <code>String</code> object</li> <li>Useful when dealing with objects created via <code>new String()</code></li> <li>Normally called automatically by JavaScript in most string operations</li> </ul> Example <pre><code>const str = new String("hello world");
console.log(str.valueOf());
</code></pre> <p>✅ Output: <pre><code>hello world
</code></pre></p> When to Use <ul> <li>When working with string objects, not primitives</li> <li>To safely extract the plain string value from a <code>String</code> object</li> <li>When comparing or serializing string objects</li> </ul>

1. 🔢 <code>.length</code> — Get String Length <ul> <li>Returns the number of characters (including spaces) in a string.</li> <li>It’s a property, not a function (no parentheses needed).</li> </ul> <pre><code> &lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 1.2em;"&gt;var str = "Hello World";&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;span style="font-weight: bold; font-size: 1.2em;"&gt;console.log(str.length); // Output: 11&lt;/span&gt;
</code></pre> 2. 🔁 <code>.repeat()</code> — Repeat a String <ul> <li>Repeats a string a specified number of times.</li> <li>Decimal values are converted to integers.</li> <li>Negative values throw an error.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "Tick tock, ";
console.log(str.repeat(2));     // Tick tock, Tick tock,
console.log(str.repeat(3.5));   // Tick tock, Tick tock, Tick tock,
console.log(str.repeat(0));     // (empty string)
</code></pre> 3. ♻️ <code>.replace()</code> — Replace First Match <ul> <li>Replaces the first match of a substring or pattern.</li> <li>Returns a new string.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const message = "bbb ccc";
let result = message.replace("b", "c");
console.log(result); // Output: "cbb ccc"
</code></pre> 4. 🔁 <code>.replaceAll()</code> — Replace All Matches <ul> <li>Replaces all occurrences of a string or pattern.</li> <li>Regex must use <code>/g</code> (global flag) or it throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day.";
console.log(str.replaceAll("day", "year"));
console.log(str.replaceAll(/day/g, "month"));
</code></pre> 5. 🔍 <code>.search()</code> — Find Index of First Match <ul> <li>Returns the index of the first match.</li> <li>Similar to <code>.match()</code> but returns a number.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const text = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
console.log(text.search("fox")); // Output: 16
</code></pre> 6. ✂️ <code>.slice()</code> — Extract a Portion of a String <ul> <li>Extracts part of a string using indices.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const fruits = ["Banana", "Orange", "Lemon", "Apple", "Mango"];
const citrus = fruits.slice(1, 3);
console.log(citrus); // [ 'Orange', 'Lemon' ]
</code></pre> 7. 🧩 <code>.split()</code> — Divide String into Array <ul> <li>Splits string into array of substrings using a delimiter.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const sentence = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day";
console.log(sentence.split());            // [ 'Carbon emission is increasing day by day' ]
console.log(sentence.split(""));          // [ 'C', 'a', 'r', ... ]
console.log(sentence.split(" "));         // [ 'Carbon', 'emission', 'is', ... ]
console.log(sentence.split("is"));        // [ 'Carbon emission ', ' increasing day by day' ]
</code></pre> 8. 🟢 <code>.startsWith()</code> — Check Start of String <ul> <li>Checks if string starts with given substring (case-sensitive).</li> </ul> <pre><code>const sentence = "Carbon emission is increasing day by day";
console.log(sentence.startsWith("Car"));     // true
console.log(sentence.startsWith("carbon"));  // false
</code></pre> 9. 📍 <code>.substring()</code> — Extract Characters by Index <ul> <li>Extracts characters between two indexes.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "Hello, Angular!";
console.log(str.substring(7));       // Angular!
console.log(str.substring(7, 13));   // Angular
</code></pre> <p>Difference vs <code>substr()</code>:</p> <pre><code>let text = "JavaScript";
console.log(text.substr(4, 6));     // Script
console.log(text.substr(-6, 6));    // Script
</code></pre> 10. 🔠 <code>.toLowerCase()</code> / <code>.toUpperCase()</code> — Change Case <ul> <li>Converts string to all lowercase or all uppercase.</li> </ul> <pre><code>let greeting = "Hello World";
console.log(greeting.toLowerCase()); // hello world
console.log(greeting.toUpperCase()); // HELLO WORLD
</code></pre> 11. 🚿 <code>.trim()</code> — Remove Whitespace <ul> <li>Removes whitespace from both ends of a string.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = "    TutorialsTonight    ";
console.log(str.trim()); // TutorialsTonight
</code></pre> 12. 🧱 <code>.valueOf()</code> — Get Primitive Value <ul> <li>Returns the primitive string value from a <code>String</code> object.</li> </ul> <pre><code>const str = new String("hello world");
console.log(str.valueOf()); // "hello world"
</code></pre>

